\f0\fs28 \cf0 #######################FDR PAPER#######################\
\
######Output of LOG-LIKELIHOODS#######\
\
This directory contains all the output files for the FDR paper. Here, we presented output \'91rst1\'92 files from CODEML, for branch-site test for positive selection. The maximum log-likelihood are imported from the \'91rst1\'92 files to evaluate the LRT, P-values and q-values. Hence, we present all the rst1 files for all the simulation analysis, real data analysis, realistic simulations, model misspecification experiments.\
\
Since the log-likelihoods are evaluated for four iterations i.e., null model, alternative model1, alternative model2 and alternative model3, we carried out codeml analysis four times. The files for simulation analysis under null hypothesis (No positive selection) are named as rst1H0null, rst1H0alt1, rst1H0alt2 and rst1H0alt3. Similarly, rst1H1null, rst1H1alt1, rst1H1alt2 and rst1H1alt3, are named for the output of codeml analysis, for simulations analysis under alternative hypothesis (with positive selection).\
\
The log-likelihood under null model and alternative model are denoted as lnL0 and lnL1. The log-likelihoods from the four iterations are stored in files prefixed with lnL0_\'85csv, lnLa1A_\'85csv, lnLa1B_\'85csv and lnLa1C_.csv. The \'91lnL1_..csv\'92 represents the log-likelihood of the alternative model, which is obtained using \'91pmax()\'92 function (in R) of all log-likelihoods, lnL0, lnLa1A, lnLa1B, lnLa1C. This means that only the largest log-likelihood among the four iterations are considered during the analysis and named as lnL1. The LRT is then calculated using lnL0 and lnL1.\
\
The above output files are provided for all the experiments/analysis carried out in this paper. Below, we define the output filenames for each analysis.\
\
1) Simulation experiment using Tree I and Tree II by specifying either alpha or beta as foreground branch\
The output rst1 files are provided in four directories-\
\
8a -  contains the output files for the simulation using Tree1 with alpha as foreground\
8b - contains the output files for the simulation using Tree1 with beta as foreground\
16a - contains the output files for the simulation with Tree2 with alpha as foreground\
16b - contains the output files for the simulation with Tree2 with beta as foreground\
\
In each of the above directories, the output files are shown for different simulations by varying the parameters of branch-site model. \
For example, \'918aw\'92 means that simulations are constructed for Tree1 by varying the omega values at the foreground alpha branch.\
As in paper, we carried out simulations at varying levels of omega = 1.5, 2, 3, 5, 8, 10.  As in above,  simulations are also constructed by varying other parameters such as \
* Proportion of sites under positive selection, p2 = 0.01, 0.05, 0.15, 0.2, 0.5 \
* Codon sequence length, lc = 100, 200, 1000, 10000.\
* Number of positive genes, g\
Hence, the directories are named as 8ap, 8alc, 8ag respectively.\
\
\
2) Real data analysis: \
Here, we analyse 9566 genes to detect positive selection at the root primate branch. The output rst1\'92files of codeml are named as rst1null, rst1alt1, rst1alt2, rst1alt3 respectively. Kosiol results are in kosiolprimatebranch_results.txt. We also provide P-values and LRTs values in separate files for reference.\
\
The \'93Kosiol_output.csv\'94 contains the results of 9,566 genes observed for positive selection at primate branch, as presented by Kosiol et al. (2008), \'93Patterns of positive selection in Six Mammalian Genomes\'94. \
\
\'93our_output.csv\'94 has the results from our reanalysis of the Kosiol primate dataset.  \
\
3) Realistic data simulation:\
In real data simulation, we removed some of the genes from among the 9,566 genes due to missing taxa within alignment. Also, we removed all genes that detected under positive selection that coincide with Kosiol\'92s results and results from our reanalysis.\
Hence, this reduces the number of genes to 6903 genes, and these are considered as neutral genes (See \'91gene_list.txt\'92). Out of these genes, 500 genes are randomly selected (see transcriptids in \'91random_sampled_500genes.txt\'92), such that they are simulated with positively selected codons. \
These randomly selected genes are modified into positively selected genes, by adding positively selected codon at end of each alignment. The target branch of positive selection is the primate branch.\
\
The degree of omega parameter varies at omega = 4 (moderate positive selection) and omega = 10. The rst1 output of codeml and log-likelihoods of 500 positively selected genes are given in directories real_data_simulation/w4 and  real_data_simulation/w10. \
\
4) Null model misspecification:\
\
The directories are named as 8species and 16species, represents the analysis for simulations using Tree-1 and Tree-II assuming beta branch as foreground. Using these trees, three different null misspecifications are tested at omega = 0.01, 0.05 and 0.1.\
The total number of simulation is 9000 replicates. \
\
The log-likelihoods obtained from four codeml iterations are stored in .csv files named as lnL0_..csv, lnL1A_..csv, lnL1B_..csv and lnL1C_..csv respectively, for each misspecification setting. The lnL1_..csv is obtained by applying pmax() to lnL0, lnL1A, lnL1B and lnL1C values. Then, lnL0 and lnL1 used to evaluate the LRT.\
\
